    <body>
      <sec id="article-26880.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Pemphigoid gestationis is a rare, autoimmune subepidermal blistering disorder that occurs during pregnancy and is characterized by intense pruritus and inflammatory skin lesions. This course details this condition, which most commonly develops during the third trimester but may arise at any point in pregnancy or in the postpartum period, including its clinical course with lesions typically beginning around the umbilical region and spreading to the abdomen and extremities, while the face is usually spared and its etiolgy, mediated by maternal IgG autoantibodies directed against hemidesmosomal proteins BP180 and BP230 within the dermal basement membrane zone. Although pemphigoid gestationis often resolves spontaneously after delivery, it may recur in future pregnancies and has been associated with preterm labor, fetal growth restriction, and neonatal skin lesions.</p>
        <p>This activity reviews the diagnostic evaluation, which relies on clinical findings supported by diagnostic testing, particularly direct immunofluorescence demonstrating linear C3 deposits at the basement membrane,&#x000a0;and the management of pemphigoid gestationis. Participants will also gain an understanding of characteristic symptoms, differentiating features from other dermatoses of pregnancy, and appropriate diagnostic tests and treatment strategies.&#x000a0;This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance the learner's competence in identifying pemphigoid gestationis, performing the recommended evaluation, assessing maternal and fetal risks, and implementing an appropriate interprofessional approach when managing this condition&#x000a0;to optimize pregnancy outcomes and patient safety.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Differentiate the&#x000a0;diagnostic features&#x000a0;used to identify pemphigoid gestationis&#x000a0;from other bullous dermatoses.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Determine&#x000a0;the complications that can occur in pregnancies affected by pemphigoid gestationis.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Identify the&#x000a0;management strategies recommended&#x000a0;for pemphigoid gestationis.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Coordinate&#x000a0;interprofessional team strategies for enhancing care coordination and&#x000a0;outcomes of pemphigoid gestationis.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=26880&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=26880">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26880.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Pemphigoid gestationis, formerly herpes gestationis, is a rare, self-limiting, autoimmune subepidermal bullous dermatosis of pregnancy. Pemphigoid gestationis was first described in 1872 by Laws Milton. This condition was previously called herpes gestationis due to the herpetiform appearance of the blisters.&#x000a0;However, the condition was not associated with herpesvirus, and the name was subsequently changed to pemphigoid gestationis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r2">[2]</xref> Pemphigoid gestationis typically presents during the third trimester, though it may occur during any trimester or the postpartum period, with inflammatory skin lesions and severe pruritus. The skin lesions commonly begin in the umbilical area and spread across the abdomen to the extremities; the face&#x000a0;is often spared. Pemphigoid gestationis recurs in 30% to 50% of subsequent pregnancies, although typically with an earlier onset and a more severe course.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>Characteristic clinical features and direct immunofluorescence are typically utilized to diagnose pemphigoid gestationis; other diagnostic studies may be performed to exclude other dermatologic conditions. Treatment comprises supportive therapies and the prevention of new skin lesions. Corticosteroids are frequently used to treat moderate-to-severe cases, though intravenous immunoglobulins have also been effective in some case studies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r2">[2]</xref> Although pemphigoid gestationis spontaneously resolves in most patients, there is evidence that the condition is associated with preterm labor, small-for-gestational-age infants, and maternal Graves' disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Due to the adverse effects associated with pemphigoid gestationis, clinicians should have a good comprehension of the&#x000a0;appropriate diagnostic tests and treatments involved with pemphigoid gestationis and the ability to effectively collaborate with an interprofessional team to improve maternal and fetal outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26880.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Pemphigoid gestationis is an immune-mediated disorder involving interactions between both innate and adaptive immune responses, with disease development primarily driven by antibody-mediated (humoral) immune mechanisms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;The skin lesions are caused by a maternal autoimmune process involving the hemidesmosomal proteins bullous pemphigoid 180 (BP180) and&#x000a0;bullous pemphigoid 230 (BP230) that are located in the dermal basement membrane zone and placental tissue. The maternal immune system identifies BP180 and BP230,&#x000a0;also known as BPAG2 and collagen XVII, as foreign proteins. However, the precise trigger for the formation of these autoantibodies is not well understood.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r2">[2]</xref> Pemphigoid gestationis is strongly associated with maternal HLA-DRB1*0301 (HLA-DR3) and HLA-DRB1*0401/040X (HLA-DR4), indicating the vital role of the major histocompatibility complex (MHC) class II in the pathogenesis of the disease. A strong association with human leukocyte antigens PG3 and PG4 also exists.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r5">[5]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26880.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Pemphigoid gestationis&#x000a0;is rare, occurring in approximately 1 in 50,000 pregnancies worldwide, with an estimated annual incidence of 0.5 to 2 cases per million individuals.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r4">[4]</xref> The incidence is estimated to range from 1 in 2,000 to 1 in 60,000 pregnancies, with most cases occurring in the second and third trimesters.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;The condition is more prevalent in multiparous than primiparous women, commonly recurring in subsequent pregnancies. However, recurrent pemphigoid gestationis typically has an earlier onset and more severe symptoms. Some studies have also reported&#x000a0;pemphigoid gestationis as a rare paraneoplastic phenomenon in association with trophoblastic tumors, including hydatidiform moles and choriocarcinomas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r4">[4]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26880.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Pemphigoid gestationis is thought to be caused by the loss of maternal immunotolerance to the placenta and fetus.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;Though the precise provocation is not well understood, maternal&#x000a0;IgG autoantibodies are produced against bullous pemphigoid protein antigens, BP180 and BP230, which are expressed in the skin, placental cytotrophoblastic and syncytiotrophoblastic cells, and amniotic membrane epithelial cells.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>Autoimmunization may occur due to intolerance to this BP 180 placental antigen, resulting in a local allogeneic reaction against the fetoplacental unit. This process, promoted by abnormal expression of placental HLA class II antigens, is accompanied by alterations in the placental basement membrane. Subsequently, an immune response in the dermoepidermal junction of the skin occurs, causing disruption of the basement membrane and the characteristic bullous skin lesions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r11">[11]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26880.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>Routine histopathologic studies are helpful for excluding other skin diseases, but their findings depend on the disease phase and severity. In the pre-bullous stage, papillary edema and infiltration of the dermis are characteristic, consisting&#x000a0;of&#x000a0;lymphocytes, histiocytes, and a variable number of eosinophils. Subepidermal blistering is usually observed in the bullous stage. However, histologic examination is typically insufficient for diagnosis because these findings are not specific to pemphigoid gestationis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Eosinophilic spongiosis&#x000a0;and epitheliotropism, with dense superficial dermal eosinophils at the dermal-epidermal junction, were diagnostic of pemphigoid gestationis in a retrospective, blinded&#x000a0;cohort study.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r12">[12]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26880.s7" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>
<bold>Clinical Course of&#x000a0;Pemphigoid Gestationis</bold>
</p>
        <p>Being knowledgeable of the characteristic clinical features of pemphigoid gestationis and the findings that differentiate it from similar skin conditions significantly assists clinicians with the diagnosis of this rare disease. Obtaining a comprehensive history, including a past and current obstetrical history, is essential since the natural clinical course of pemphigoid gestationis can involve exacerbations and remissions, with flares being common during the parturition and postpartum periods.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>Pemphigoid gestationis usually resolves spontaneously within 1 to 2 months after delivery. However, symptoms may persist or even worsen after pregnancy due to a sudden increase in antibody levels. Furthermore, recurrence during any subsequent pregnancy (eg, miscarriage and abortion), or less commonly with menstrual cycles or combination oral contraceptive use, is well established. Fetal risks have also been observed in pregnancies with pemphigoid gestationis, including fetal growth restriction, preterm labor, and temporary neonatal skin lesions, which usually resolve several weeks after birth. This risk to the pregnancy appears to be correlated with disease severity; however, no increased risk of stillbirth or miscarriage has been observed. Therefore, obtaining a complete history helps to guide clinicians when considering differential diagnoses.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Clinical Features of&#x000a0;Pemphigoid Gestationis</bold>
</p>
        <p>Pruritus is the primary clinical symptom, which may precede skin manifestations in some patients. Because lesions progress through different stages, clinical features can vary. The polymorphic eruptions can include eczematous or erythema multiforme-like lesions, erythematous urticarial plaques, and papules, which progress to vesicles, tense blisters, and bullae in more than 65% of cases (see&#x000a0;<bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Pemphigoid Gestationis). The lesions&#x000a0;can vary in severity and have a significant psychological impact on the patient.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>Initially, the lesions erupt in the umbilical area and subsequently spread to other parts of the abdominal region and the extremities, including the thighs, palms, and soles (see <bold>Image.</bold> Abdominal Pemphigoid Gestationis&#x000a0;Lesions).&#x000a0;Clustered vesicles and bullae typically arise beginning in the umbilical region and spreading to the trunk, extremities, and sometimes the palms and soles.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>The face and mucosal membranes are often spared; mucous membrane involvement occurs in only about 20% of cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;The physical examination findings and distribution of the skin&#x000a0;lesions are frequently nonspecific during the initial phase; clinicians may have difficulty distinguishing pemphigoid gestationis from other&#x000a0;pruriginous dermatoses of pregnancy, especially the polymorphic eruption of pregnancy (PEP), also known as polymorphic urticarial papules and plaques of pregnancy (PUPPP). PEP can also develop in the third trimester of pregnancy and exhibit similar clinical features to pemphigoid gestationis. Because the differential diagnoses are challenging to differentiate symptomatically and histopathologically in the pre-bullous stage, direct immunofluorescence is typically used to confirm the diagnosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26880.s8" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Because clinical features alone are nonspecific, diagnostic studies, including histopathology, direct immunofluorescence, and enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA), are frequently used to diagnose pemphigoid gestationis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;However, direct immunofluorescence (DIF) staining of skin biopsy specimens is considered the most sensitive and specific diagnostic test for confirming pemphigoid gestationis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Characteristic findings of pemphigoid gestationis on DIF include linear C3 deposits and IgG autoantibodies at the basement membrane zone. C3 deposits are considered diagnostic of pemphigoid gestationis, as this has been observed in all DIF stains of patients with pemphigoid gestationis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Interestingly, DIF sometimes remains positive for 6 months to 4 years after clinical remission.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>In addition to DIF, several other studies can be used to diagnose pemphigoid gestationis. Routine histopathologic studies are helpful in excluding other skin diseases, but the findings depend on the phase and severity of the disease. In the pre-bullous stage, papillary edema and infiltration of the dermis are characteristic, consisting&#x000a0;of&#x000a0;lymphocytes, histiocytes, and a variable number of eosinophils.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r3">[3]</xref> Immunochemistry studies in patients with pemphigoid gestationis show C4d deposits along the dermal-epidermal junction. Indirect immunofluorescence microscopy detects circulating IgG antibodies in the serum of approximately 30% to 100% of patients with pemphigoid gestationis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;ELISA testing distinguishes pemphigoid gestationis from other pregnancy dermatoses by identifying BP180 IgG antibodies in serum specimens. ELISA reportedly has high sensitivity and specificity of approximately 95%, which may render direct immunofluorescence,&#x000a0;currently the reference standard, obsolete. Limitations of both DIF and indirect immunofluorescence include the need for nonformalin tissue fixation and appropriately equipped laboratory facilities, as well as increased costs, which may be difficult to meet in developing countries.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r14">[14]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26880.s9" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Treatment of pemphigoid gestationis depends on the severity and the stage of the skin lesions. The main goal is to relieve itching and prevent the formation of new blisters. The mainstay of treatment is topical high-potency corticosteroids and antihistamines. However, additional therapies may be needed for patients unresponsive to initial therapy. More recent treatment regimens have been utilized to reduce the use of high-dose steroids and prevent potential complications in pregnancy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>Although most pemphigoid gestationis treatment recommendations currently come from case studies, topical corticosteroids and oral antihistamines have typically been preferred, especially when the disease is localized and minimal blistering is present.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;Patients resistant to topical treatment or with a severe form involving more than 10% of the body surface area may require systemic corticosteroids. Treatment&#x000a0;includes oral prednisolone 0.5 mg/kg daily, with a recommended total daily dose of less than 20 mg. Prednisone and prednisolone are preferred over other agents because they cross the placenta at lower concentrations.</p>
        <p>If no new blisters occur within 2 weeks, treatment is considered successful, and clinicians should taper patients off of corticosteroid therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;For nonresponsive patients,&#x000a0;immunosuppressant agents, eg, cyclosporine, dapsone, or azathioprine, are considered for disease control; however, clinicians should be aware of potential adverse effects with these medications (eg, bone marrow suppression, preterm birth, and liver impairment).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Intravenous immunoglobulins (IVIG) have more recently been used successfully, alone or in combination with other agents, and have a better safety profile than immunosuppressants.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Dupilumab, a monoclonal antibody that targets the alpha subunit of the interleukin-4 receptor, has most recently been used as a biologic therapy, particularly in refractory and corticosteroid-dependent bullous pemphigoid. This seems to be a new and promising therapy, especially in women with comorbidities,&#x000a0;eg, diabetes, where systemic steroid therapy may be contraindicated. Dupilumab use was associated with rapid remission of symptoms and the ability to wean from high-dose systemic steroid use.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;Additionally, rituximab has reportedly been effective in preventing recurrent pemphigoid gestationis when given preconceptually. Patients should avoid a subsequent pregnancy for 12 months following the last infusion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26880.s10" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Other conditions that should be considered when evaluating patients for pemphigoid gestationis include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Polymorphic eruption of pregnancy (PEP)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Urticaria</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Allergic contact dermatitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Bullous pemphigoid</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cicatricial pemphigoid</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Dermatitis herpetiformis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Erythema multiforme</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Impetigo</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Linear IgA dermatosis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Bullous drug eruption</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Atopic eruption of pregnancy&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r2">[2]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Pemphigoid gestationis may be most easily confused with PEP because both involve edematous wheals or urticated papules that are less than 1 cm and plaques over 1 cm on the arms, legs, and abdomen.&#x000a0;Distinguishing between PEP and pemphigoid gestationis is clinically important because PEP is not linked to meaningful maternal or fetal complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r22">[22]</xref> In contrast, pemphigoid gestationis carries important risks, including preterm birth, fetal growth restriction related to placental dysfunction, transient neonatal skin lesions from transplacental maternal antibodies, and a high likelihood of recurrence in subsequent pregnancies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r23">[23]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>The Pregnancy Dermatoses Clinical Scoring System</bold>
</p>
        <p>The pregnancy dermatoses clinical scoring system has been proposed as a practical tool to help distinguish pemphigoid gestationis from PEP using clinical history and examination findings. The score incorporates key features associated with each condition and points assigned as follows:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Prior history of pemphigoid gestationis (+2)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Primigravidity (&#x02212;1)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Timing of rash onset
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Onset before 36 weeks assigned (+1)&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>Onset before 28 weeks assigned (+2)</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Umbilical involvement (+2)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Presence of blisters greater than 1 cm (+2)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Eruptions confined to or arising from abdominal striae (&#x02212;2)</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>A total score of 3 or higher indicates a high probability of pemphigoid gestationis and may help guide further diagnostic evaluation and management in clinical practice.&#x000a0;Because both pemphigoid gestationis and polymorphic eruption of pregnancy commonly present with similar skin findings,&#x000a0;eg, erythematous urticarial papules, plaques, and small vesicles involving the trunk and extremities, these shared morphologic features were not included in the scoring system, as they do not reliably distinguish between the&#x000a0;conditions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;The pregnancy dermatoses clinical scoring system is not intended to replace definitive diagnostic testing,&#x000a0;eg, direct immunofluorescence demonstrating linear C3 deposition or ELISA testing for BP180 antibodies. However, it may serve as a useful clinical tool in settings where access to specialized diagnostic testing is limited.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r22">[22]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26880.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Pemphigoid gestationis generally carries a favorable prognosis. Recurrence occurs more frequently during subsequent pregnancies, often with earlier onset or increased severity. Women who experience a pregnancy affected by pemphigoid gestationis also face a higher likelihood of developing other autoimmune conditions later in life, particularly Graves disease. In approximately 10% of affected pregnancies, neonatal pemphigoid develops with transient neonatal blistering caused by transplacental transfer of maternal antibodies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r4">[4]</xref> These neonatal skin lesions typically resolve spontaneously without long-term complications.</p>
        <p>Potential pregnancy-related risks include spontaneous abortion, preterm labor and delivery, intrauterine growth restriction, and low birth weight; however, recent studies indicate that such outcomes remain relatively uncommon.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r2">[2]</xref> Reduction of fetal risk depends heavily on timely clinical recognition and confirmation through histopathological evaluation. Careful prenatal monitoring should include screening for intrauterine fetal growth restriction and surveillance for complications related to high-dose corticosteroid therapy. Effective management benefits from an interprofessional care model involving specialists in neonatology, pediatrics, obstetrics, and dermatology, which supports maternal health and neonatal well-being during pregnancy, after delivery, and in subsequent pregnancies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r2">[2]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26880.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Pemphigoid gestationis generally involves limited maternal and fetal complications; however, the associated pruritus can become severe and debilitating. Long-term corticosteroid therapy during pregnancy carries potential risks, including preterm birth, preeclampsia, preterm premature rupture of membranes, venous thromboembolism, possible congenital anomalies, pyelonephritis, and potential intrauterine fetal death.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r24">[24]</xref> Pregnancies exposed to prolonged corticosteroid therapy also demonstrate a higher incidence of small-for-gestational-age infants. A definitive causal relationship between corticosteroid therapy and fetal growth restriction remains uncertain, since the underlying maternal condition requiring treatment may also contribute to adverse outcomes.</p>
        <p>Evidence from certain animal studies suggests an association between corticosteroid exposure and abnormal placentation, which may subsequently contribute to fetal growth restriction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26880.r24">[24]</xref> The precise biological mechanisms underlying this association remain incompletely understood. Careful evaluation of potential risks and therapeutic benefits must guide treatment strategies that involve prolonged corticosteroid use during pregnancy. Additional research remains necessary to determine whether children born to mothers treated with corticosteroids during pregnancy experience any long-term health effects.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26880.s13" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Deterrence of pemphigoid gestationis focuses on early recognition, prompt diagnosis, and timely management to reduce complications for both the mother and fetus. Although the condition cannot be prevented, clinicians should educate pregnant patients to promptly report persistent pruritus or the development of new skin lesions, particularly those beginning around the umbilical region. These symptoms may precede more extensive blistering eruptions and require clinical evaluation. Patient education should also include information about the autoimmune nature of the disorder, the typical disease course, and the possibility of recurrence in future pregnancies or postpartum flares.</p>
        <p>Patient counseling should emphasize adherence to prescribed therapies, including topical or systemic corticosteroids and supportive treatments to reduce pruritus and prevent new blister formation. Patients should also be informed about potential pregnancy-related risks, such as preterm birth or fetal growth restriction, as well as the association with maternal autoimmune conditions. Clear instructions regarding monitoring symptoms, attending follow-up appointments, and maintaining communication with the healthcare team support effective care. When combined with coordinated interprofessional care, comprehensive patient education promotes timely intervention, improves patient engagement, and contributes to better maternal and neonatal outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26880.s14" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Pemphigoid gestationis is a rare autoimmune blistering disorder that occurs during pregnancy and is characterized by intense pruritus and inflammatory skin eruptions. The condition typically develops during the third trimester but may occur at any time during pregnancy or in the postpartum period. Lesions often begin around the umbilical region and spread to the abdomen and extremities while sparing the face. Pemphigoid gestationis results from maternal IgG autoantibodies targeting hemidesmosomal proteins BP180 and BP230 within the dermal basement membrane. Diagnosis relies on clinical assessment supported by diagnostic testing, particularly direct immunofluorescence demonstrating linear C3 deposition along the basement membrane. Although the condition usually resolves spontaneously after delivery, it may recur in subsequent pregnancies and has been associated with fetal growth restriction, preterm birth, and transient neonatal skin lesions.</p>
        <p>Effective management requires coordinated interprofessional care to ensure accurate diagnosis, symptom control, and monitoring for maternal and fetal complications. Physicians, general practitioners, and advanced practitioners are responsible for recognizing characteristic features, ordering appropriate diagnostic tests, and initiating evidence-based treatment such as topical or systemic corticosteroids. Nurses play a critical role in patient education, monitoring symptom progression, and assessing treatment response, while pharmacists help guide safe medication use and monitor potential drug-related adverse effects during pregnancy. Collaboration among dermatologists, obstetric providers, and other healthcare professionals supports timely communication, individualized treatment planning, and ongoing maternal&#x02013;fetal monitoring. Through effective care coordination and shared decision-making, the healthcare team can improve patient-centered care, enhance patient safety, and optimize maternal and neonatal outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
